  "id": "COMPOUND:3261 - PROTEIN:1477",
  "verbalization": "Sparfosic acid appears to interact with the Sodium- and chloride-dependent GABA transporter 1 through a series of molecular pathways. The evidence suggests that Sparfosic acid targets Potassium channel subfamily K member 3, which is a target of multiple compounds, including Benzofuran-6-carbaldehyde and (Z)-N-[(4-hydroxy-3-methoxyphenyl)methyl]-9-methyldec-7-enamide. These latter compounds are also targets of Sparfosic acid, leading to a chain of interactions that ultimately result in the activation of the GABA transporter 1. This suggests a potential mechanism where Sparfosic acid may modulate the activity of potassium channels, which in turn influence the function of the GABA transporter. The evidence supports a pathway where Sparfosic acid's effects are mediated through potassium channel activity, which is a recurring mechanism across the visible paths. The integration of these findings highlights the importance of potassium channels in the regulation of the GABA transporter, offering a plausible explanation for the observed interactions.",
